BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Appreciation vs Appropriation: Reconciliation in Practice
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260921T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260921T193000
UID:6a87439baafa6100295decf9@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join Dr. Ruth Green for a discussion that explores the line be
 tween Indigenous cultural appreciation and cultural appropriation. Using r
 eal world examples like artwork\, jewelry\, clothing\, and pow wows\, Ruth
  will examine how non-Indigenous people can engage respectfully with Indig
 enous cultures and make informed\, respectful choices. Attendees will lear
 n how to reflect on representation\, ownership\, intent\, and ceremony\, w
 hile preparing to thoughtfully respond to questions about their choices.\n
 No registration required. All are welcome.\nDr. Ruth Green (she/her) is a 
 Haudenosaunee urban Indigequeer woman (Kanienkehaka Turtle Clan from Six N
 ations) who identifies as an activist-turned-accidental academic. She is a
 n Associate Professor in the Indigenous Field of Study Program at Wilfrid 
 Laurier University. Previously Ruth was chair of the Indigenous Research E
 thics Board\, special advisor to the President and the Dean on Indigenous 
 Initiatives and Director of the School of Social Work at York University. 
 She is a board member of Pride Toronto and a Community Council member with
  Aboriginal Legal Services
LOCATION:Spadina Road
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a87439baafa6100295decf9
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
