BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Storytelling Through Beadwork
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260916T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260916T200000
UID:6a73b7ae9006f753531532a4@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Uncover the hidden symbols and intricate meanings found in bea
 dwork in this interactive talk with Cree-Métis artist\, Marissa Magneson.
 \nMarissa will explore beadwork as a form of storytelling and expression\,
  revealing layers of history\, identity\, culture\, and spirituality.\nAtt
 endees will gain a deeper appreciation for this art form that transforms 
 beads into vibrant narratives!\nMarissa Magneson is a Cree-Métis artist\,
  photographer\, and educator with Red River roots\, holding a BFA (Hons.) 
 and a Master’s in Indigenous Studies with a focus on beadwork as visual 
 storytelling. Her work centers on decolonizing education\, community stren
 gthening\, and reclaiming cultural practices\, and she has been nationally
  recognized as a 2023 Vanier Scholar and one of York University’s Top 30
  Alumni Under 30. Currently a PhD candidate at York University\, Marissa a
 lso teaches\, creates\, and facilitates workshops. She is inspired by the 
 legacy of her Métis grandmother\, Elaine Jessop (Desjarlais).\nInformatio
 n Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected b
 y the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(
 a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and pr
 ogram registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the libr
 ary handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at answer
 line@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8
LOCATION:Lillian H. Smith\, Room B/C
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a73b7ae9006f753531532a4
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
