BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Movie Matinee: Some Like It Hot (1959)
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260911T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260911T160000
UID:6a90d057b2047800299619bb@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join us for an afternoon screening of the 1959 classic\nSome L
 ike It Hot\n!\n“Billy Wilder’s zany cross-dressing comedy begins with 
 a massacre – resembling the gangland St Valentine’s Day killings of 19
 29 – and ends with one of the most celebrated last lines in cinema histo
 ry. Written in cahoots with the director’s new collaborator I.A.L. Diamo
 nd\,\nSome Like It Hot\nascends to inspired heights of silliness in-betwee
 n\, with Tony Curtis and Jack Lemmon both on career-best form as dragged-u
 p musicians hiding out with Sugar Kane’s girl band.\nBoth the gangster s
 tory and the screwball antics hark back to Hollywood films of the 1930s\, 
 but Wilder’s outrageous and subversive play with gender was truly bounda
 ry pushing and helped lead to a loosening of censorship after United Artis
 ts released the film without certification.” – British Film Institute\
 nRuntime: 2 hours and 1 minute.
LOCATION:Forest Hill\, Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a90d057b2047800299619bb
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Branch Art Exhibit: Smaller and More Robotic
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260901T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260930T235959
UID:6a91bb035e6fc10d0550658d@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Throughout the month of September\, the works of artist Jay R
 angam are on display at Oakwood Village Library Arts Centre.\nJay Rangam 
 is an artist that lives and works in Toronto\, Canada. He received his MFA
  from the Maryland Institute College of Art in 2021 and his BFA\nfrom OCAD
  University in 2019. He has exhibited in group shows in Toronto\, Montreal
 \, Baltimore and New York.\nMy recent paintings from 2024 - 2025 begin wit
 h digitally combining drawings that I’ve used in previous bodies of work
  to create new images. Using\nthe digital cartoon as a starting point for 
 painting\, the application of different materials and colours is intended 
 to abstract the representational subject\nmatter of the drawings\, creatin
 g paintings that appear somewhat abstract while retaining traces of a rela
 tionship to the natural world.\nThis exhibit is part of TPL's\nArt Exhibit
  Space\nprogram. Toronto Public Library provides space for local artists t
 o exhibit their work at locations across the city. These exhibits aim to r
 eflect the diverse cultural interests of the city and its neighbourhoods.\
 nThe exhibit can be viewed during Deer Park's open hours. Please check the
  Oakwood Village branch page before your visit. The branch will be closed
  on September 6th\, 7th\, and 30th.\nFree. All are welcome.\nTitle of Piec
 e: Garden\nMedium (e.g. oil\, acrylic\,\nphotography\, etc.): gouache on c
 anvas\nSize/Dimensions: Extra Large
LOCATION:Oakwood Village Library and Arts Centre
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a91bb035e6fc10d0550658d
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Bring Your Problem: Can AI Help?
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261104T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261104T200000
UID:6a9b519baafa61002961b295@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Do you have a recurring problem you think AI might help with? 
 A thing you keep doing by hand that feels like it should be easier? Bring 
 it. If you don't have one\, come help your neighbours with theirs.\nIn thi
 s interactive class\, you'll learn a simple seven-step way to think about 
 problems: what's annoying you\, what you've tried\, what AI could actually
  do\, and where it would fail. We'll gather real problems from the room\, 
 sort them into groups\, and map out what's worth solving with AI\, and wha
 t's better solved without it. We'll also talk honestly about what an AI fi
 x costs: your money\, your time\, and sometimes your private information. 
 The problems AI could help with most are often the most personal\, and the
  most personal ones are worth the most care.\nRegistration is required and
  will start October 9\, 2026 at 12:30 PM.\nNote: Your spot will not be gua
 ranteed after 5 minutes of the start of the program.\nInformation Collecti
 on Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Toro
 nto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)
 \, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program regi
 stration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handle
 s your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.
 ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, 2nd Floor eLearning Lab
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a9b519baafa61002961b295
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:AI Tinkerers Show and Tell
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261024T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261024T160000
UID:6a9b4e352dac6e003724371d@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:What are people actually building with AI\, not in the news bu
 t in your city\, by your neighbours?\nJoin members of the AI Tinkerers and
  the Human Feedback Foundation communities for an afternoon of live demos 
 and projects at the library. See what local builders are working on\, ask 
 questions\, and get ideas for your own experiments. Real projects\, real p
 eople\, no product pitches. A window into what's possible when curious peo
 ple get their hands on AI.\nRegistration is required and will start Septem
 ber 9\, 2026 at 9:00 AM.\nNote: Your spot will not be guaranteed after 5 m
 inutes of the start of the program.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe p
 ersonal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Librar
 y under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The
  information will only be used for event and program registration\, delive
 ry and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your informatio
 n can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-59
 81\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, 2nd Floor Auditorium
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a9b4e352dac6e003724371d
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:How Do AIs "Think"? And Why They Get Things Wrong
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261015T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261015T200000
UID:6a9b4e29aafa61002961b09d@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:How does a machine actually "think"? Explore the history and l
 atest developments of machine learning.\nIn this class\, we'll dig into wh
 at's really going on inside AI systems: how they decide what matters in yo
 ur question\, where their training data comes from (and who's missing from
  it)\, and how hidden instructions can sneak into content and hijack an AI
 . Develop an intuitive understanding of how AIs "think" without the math. 
 We'll talk about how AIs remember\, how they don't and how they are both s
 imilar and different to humans. And we'll ask the question underneath it a
 ll: is there anyone home inside the machine?\nRegistration is required and
  will start September 9\, 2026 at 9:00 AM.\nNote: Your spot will not be gu
 aranteed after 5 minutes of the start of the program.\nInformation Collect
 ion Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Tor
 onto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c
 )\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program reg
 istration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handl
 es your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl
 .ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, 2nd Floor eLearning Lab
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a9b4e29aafa61002961b09d
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:AI for Us: Shaping AI in Toronto
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261205T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261205T160000
UID:6a9b51beaafa61002961b2a7@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Toronto is one of the biggest AI cities in the world. But what
  do the people here actually think about it and how do we want to shape it
 s development and use?\nA panel of experts\, who know AI from very differe
 nt angles\, united for an open conversation about what AI is doing to this
  city\, and what we want it to do instead. We'll talk about:\nThe human be
 ings behind the machines: what drives the people who build AI\, and what w
 orries them.\nWork\, money\, and meaning: what happens to jobs\, creativit
 y\, and value when AI lands here.\nWho gets to shape it and who's left out
  (both are our neighbours).\nThe stories we tell: how art\, media\, news\,
  and everyday talk shape what we expect\, fear\, and hope for.\nNo pitches
 \, no boosterism. Just an honest conversation about the ideas and stories 
 shaping AI in this city\, and what we want it to become.\nFeaturing:\nJerr
 old McGrath\n\, Cultural Programmer and Theorist\, In the Meantime\nJoseph
  Wilson\n\, Anthropologist & Author of Humans of AI: Understanding the Hum
 ans Behind the Machines\nOwais Lightwala\n\, Assistant Professor\, Perform
 ance at The Creative School at Toronto Metropolitan University\, Director\
 ,\nChrysalis\nRegistration is required and will start November 9\, 2026 at
  12:30 PM.\nNote: Your spot will not be guaranteed after 5 minutes of the 
 start of the program.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, Room 2/3
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a9b51beaafa61002961b2a7
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Pressed Flowers Necklace Workshop
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260926T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260926T160000
UID:6a135ba1f6bd012f006a0d97@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Capture summer’s beauty forever in this hands-on Flower Pres
 sed Necklace Workshop\, hosted by Gabriela Delworth. Whether you're a jewe
 lry enthusiast or a nature lover\, this session offers a creative space to
  design a wearable piece of art that lasts forever.\nWhat You Will Discove
 r:\nThe Art of Pressing: Learn the essential techniques for preserving blo
 oms\, including the best time of day to harvest and which flower varieties
  hold their color and shape best.\nStorage Secrets: Master the professiona
 l methods for storing pressed flowers to prevent fading and moisture damag
 e.\nJewelry Design: Arrange your choice of delicate petals and greenery in
 to a custom pendant. You will leave with a one-of-a-kind necklace that res
 pects your personal style.\nWorkshop Details:\nAll inclusive: Your free re
 gistration covers all materials\; all participants receive a curated kit b
 y the instructor. Tools are provided by the instructor and returned at the
  end of the workshop.\nSkill Level: Perfect for beginners\; no prior exper
 ience in jewelry making or gardening is required.
LOCATION:Forest Hill\, Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a135ba1f6bd012f006a0d97
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
